                    O and
                    δ
                    D of river water ranged from −13.2‰ to −8.6‰ and from −88.0‰ to −56.7‰, respectively. Meteorological data revealed that subcloud evaporation on precipitation at low elevation is stronger than that at high elevation. Combined with the spatial variations of subcloud evaporation effect on precipitation, melting water recharge and evaporation are identified as the main factors influencing the stable isotopic compositions of river water across the Bailong River catchment. We established a
                    δ
                    18
                    O lapse rate of 1.4‰ km
                    −1
                    for the Bailong River, which is much lower than that of other rivers in the eastern Tibetan Plateau. Moreover, the isotopic lapse rate of river water in the eastern Tibetan Plateau gradually decreases with increasing latitude, which is attributed to the contribution of evapotranspiration to precipitation. As an important hydrological process, moisture recycling plays a crucial role in shaping the isotopic compositions of precipitation and thereby affects the isotopic lapse rate. The atmospheric circulation patterns over the Tibetan Plateau are changing due to global warming, which further influences isotopic lapse rate. Therefore, it is urgent to assess the extent to which changes in the source of water vapor driven by global warming have impacted the isotopic lapse rate.
                  
                  
                    Significance Statement
                    The purpose of this study is to improve the understanding of hydrological processes in the northeastern Tibetan Plateau. Our results showed that moisture recycling is the main factor that influences the isotopic lapse rate of river water. However, the atmospheric circulation patterns over the Tibetan Plateau are changing due to global warming, influencing the accuracy of reconstructing the paleoelevation based on the isotopic lapse rate. We therefore propose that it is urgent to evaluate how global warming alters water vapor sources and impacts the isotopic lapse rate.
